================================================================================
                    COMMON MISTAKES TO AVOID
================================================================================

❌ MISTAKE 1: Hardcoding secrets
  ❌ const JWT_SECRET = 'mysecret';
  ✅ const JWT_SECRET = process.env.JWT_SECRET;

❌ MISTAKE 2: Not validating at all
  ❌ const id = req.query.id;
  ✅ const id = parseInt(req.query.id, 10); if (isNaN(id)) return error;

❌ MISTAKE 3: Trusting client-side validation
  ❌ if (data.length > 100) { // This was validated in browser }
  ✅ Always validate on server

❌ MISTAKE 4: Logging sensitive data
  ❌ logger.info({ password: req.body.password });
  ✅ logger.info({ user: req.user.id });

❌ MISTAKE 5: Using synchronous crypto
  ❌ crypto.pbkdfSync(...) in route handler blocks all requests
  ✅ Use async crypto or worker threads

❌ MISTAKE 6: Not setting secure cookie flags
  ❌ res.cookie('token', token);
  ✅ res.cookie('token', token, {secure: true, httpOnly: true, sameSite: true});

❌ MISTAKE 7: Exposing error details
  ❌ res.json({error: 'User table join failed: ...'});
  ✅ res.json({error: 'Internal server error'});

❌ MISTAKE 8: Empty try/catch blocks
  ❌ try { ... } catch(e) {}
  ✅ try { ... } catch(e) { logger.error(e); res.status(500).json(...); }

❌ MISTAKE 9: Not rotating secrets
  ❌ Use same JWT_SECRET forever
  ✅ Rotate keys periodically

❌ MISTAKE 10: Ignoring dependency vulnerabilities
  ❌ npm audit shows 10 vulnerabilities, ignore it
  ✅ npm audit fix && npm audit && review changes


================================================================================
                    SECURITY BEST PRACTICES
================================================================================

1. Principle of Least Privilege
   - Users only get minimum necessary permissions
   - Don't make everyone admin
   - Default deny, explicit allow

2. Defense in Depth
   - Multiple layers of security
   - Don't rely on one control
   - Client-side AND server-side validation

3. Fail Secure
   - Default to denying access
   - Never accidentally allow
   - Test failure cases

4. Complete Mediation
   - Check every request
   - Don't cache auth results long-term
   - Always validate at boundaries

5. Open Design
   - Security through transparency
   - Good security practices are public
   - No "security through obscurity"

6. Separation of Concerns
   - Authentication separate from authorization
   - Logging separate from business logic
   - Error handling separate from routing

7. Auditability
   - Log security events
   - Maintain audit trails
   - Enable forensics

8. Minimal Attack Surface
   - Only expose needed endpoints
   - Hide implementation details
   - Use security headers


================================================================================

💡 TIP: Start with the Quick Fixes above, then move to the full app_secure.js
       implementation as you gain confidence.

