============================================================================
SECRETS MANAGEMENT & ROTATION STRATEGY
============================================================================

⚠️  IMPORTANT SECURITY NOTE:
============================
The current application loads secrets from .env files. This is convenient
for development but creates several security risks in production:

1. Secrets in .env files can be accidentally committed to git
2. Secrets are stored in plain text on the server
3. No audit trail of who accessed which secret
4. No support for key rotation
5. Vulnerable to compromise if server is breached

SECRETS INVENTORY (From .env.example):
=====================================

Identified Sensitive Variables:
├─ JWT_SECRET          (Cryptographic key for token signing)
├─ DB_PASSWORD         (Database authentication credential)
├─ DB_USER             (Database username)
├─ DB_HOST             (Database connection details)
├─ DB_NAME             (Database name)
├─ LOG_FILE            (Application logging path)
└─ CORS_ORIGIN         (Security-relevant configuration)

Risk Assessment:
┌──────────────────────────────────────┬──────────┬──────────┐
│ Secret                               │ Severity │ Impact   │
├──────────────────────────────────────┼──────────┼──────────┤
│ JWT_SECRET                           │ CRITICAL │ Auth     │
│ DB_PASSWORD                          │ CRITICAL │ Data     │
│ DB_USER                              │ HIGH     │ Data     │
│ Database connection strings          │ HIGH     │ Exposure │
└──────────────────────────────────────┴──────────┴──────────┘

SOLUTION: MIGRATE TO A SECRETS MANAGER
======================================

Option 1: AWS Secrets Manager (Recommended for AWS)
────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Pros:
  ✓ Automatic key rotation
  ✓ Audit logging
  ✓ Fine-grained access control
  ✓ Encryption at rest and in transit
  ✓ Integrates with Lambda, ECS, EC2

Implementation:
```javascript
const AWS = require('aws-sdk');
const secretsManager = new AWS.SecretsManager();

async function getSecret(secretName) {
  try {
    const data = await secretsManager
      .getSecretValue({ SecretId: secretName })
      .promise();

    if ('SecretString' in data) {
      return JSON.parse(data.SecretString);
    } else {
      return Buffer.from(data.SecretBinary, 'base64').toString('ascii');
    }
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('Error retrieving secret:', error);
    throw error;
  }
}

// Usage:
const secrets = await getSecret('prod/app/secrets');
const JWT_SECRET = secrets.JWT_SECRET;
const DB_PASSWORD = secrets.DB_PASSWORD;
```

Installation:
```bash
npm install aws-sdk
```

Option 2: Azure Key Vault (Recommended for Azure)
───────────────────────────────────────────────────
Pros:
  ✓ Cloud-agnostic
  ✓ HSM-backed encryption
  ✓ Automatic key rotation policies
  ✓ Access logging and monitoring
  ✓ Compliance certifications (HIPAA, PCI-DSS, SOC 2)

Implementation:
```javascript
const { SecretClient } = require("@azure/keyvault-secrets");
const { DefaultAzureCredential } = require("@azure/identity");

const credential = new DefaultAzureCredential();
const client = new SecretClient(
  `https://<vault-name>.vault.azure.net/`,
  credential
);

async function getSecret(secretName) {
  const secret = await client.getSecret(secretName);
  return secret.value;
}

// Usage:
const JWT_SECRET = await getSecret("jwt-secret");
const DB_PASSWORD = await getSecret("db-password");
```

Installation:
```bash
npm install @azure/keyvault-secrets @azure/identity
```

Option 3: HashiCorp Vault (Recommended for self-hosted)
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Pros:
  ✓ Self-hosted control
  ✓ Dynamic secrets generation
  ✓ Automatic credential rotation
  ✓ Multi-cloud support
  ✓ Detailed audit logging
  ✓ Role-based access control

Implementation:
```javascript
const vault = require('node-vault')({
  endpoint: process.env.VAULT_ADDR,
  token: process.env.VAULT_TOKEN
});

async function getSecret(path, key) {
  try {
    const secret = await vault.read(path);
    return secret.data.data[key];
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('Vault error:', error);
    throw error;
  }
}

// Usage:
const JWT_SECRET = await getSecret('secret/app/prod', 'jwt_secret');
const DB_PASSWORD = await getSecret('secret/app/prod', 'db_password');
```

Installation:
```bash
npm install node-vault
```

Option 4: Docker Secrets (For Docker Swarm)
─────────────────────────────────────────────
Best for containerized applications in Docker Swarm.

Usage:
```javascript
const fs = require('fs');

function getSecret(secretName) {
  try {
    return fs.readFileSync(`/run/secrets/${secretName}`, 'utf8').trim();
  } catch (error) {
    console.error(`Secret ${secretName} not found:`, error);
    throw error;
  }
}

// Usage:
const JWT_SECRET = getSecret('jwt_secret');
const DB_PASSWORD = getSecret('db_password');
```

Option 5: Kubernetes Secrets (For Kubernetes)
──────────────────────────────────────────────
Best for Kubernetes deployments.

Create secrets:
```bash
kubectl create secret generic app-secrets \
  --from-literal=JWT_SECRET=your-secret \
  --from-literal=DB_PASSWORD=your-password
```

Access in pod:
```javascript
const fs = require('fs');

function getKubernetesSecret(secretName) {
  const path = `/var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/${secretName}`;
  return fs.readFileSync(path, 'utf8');
}
```

MIGRATION PLAN: .env → Secrets Manager
=======================================

Phase 1: Setup (1 hour)
├─ Choose secrets manager (AWS, Azure, HashiCorp, etc.)
├─ Create service account/IAM role with minimal permissions
├─ Store all secrets in the chosen manager
└─ Test access from development environment

Phase 2: Implementation (2-3 hours)
├─ Update config.js to use secrets manager
├─ Create wrapper functions for secret retrieval
├─ Update app_secure.js to use new config
├─ Add error handling for secret retrieval failures
└─ Test all endpoints with new configuration

Phase 3: Testing (1-2 hours)
├─ Verify all secrets load correctly
├─ Test fallback behavior if secret unavailable
├─ Verify audit logs are created
├─ Test secret rotation doesn't break application
└─ Load test with new secrets manager

Phase 4: Deployment (1-2 hours)
├─ Update CI/CD pipeline to not inject .env
├─ Deploy to staging with secrets manager
├─ Validate production access with secrets manager
├─ Deploy to production
└─ Monitor for errors/slow secret retrieval

UPDATED config.js (Using Secrets Manager)
==========================================

```javascript
require('dotenv').config({ override: false }); // Dev fallback only

const AWS = require('aws-sdk');
let cachedSecrets = null;

async function loadSecrets() {
  const env = process.env.NODE_ENV || 'development';

  // In development, use .env files
  if (env === 'development') {
    return {
      jwt_secret: process.env.JWT_SECRET,
      db_password: process.env.DB_PASSWORD,
      db_user: process.env.DB_USER,
      db_host: process.env.DB_HOST,
      db_name: process.env.DB_NAME,
    };
  }

  // In production, use AWS Secrets Manager
  if (cachedSecrets) {
    return cachedSecrets;
  }

  try {
    const secretsManager = new AWS.SecretsManager();
    const data = await secretsManager
      .getSecretValue({ SecretId: `${env}/app/secrets` })
      .promise();

    cachedSecrets = JSON.parse(data.SecretString);

    // Refresh every 1 hour
    setTimeout(() => {
      cachedSecrets = null;
    }, 60 * 60 * 1000);

    return cachedSecrets;
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('Failed to load secrets from AWS:', error);
    throw new Error('Cannot start application without secrets');
  }
}

module.exports = loadSecrets;
```

Updated app_secure.js startup:
```javascript
const loadSecrets = require('./config');

async function startServer() {
  try {
    const secrets = await loadSecrets();

    const JWT_SECRET = secrets.jwt_secret;
    const DB_PASSWORD = secrets.db_password;

    // Initialize app...
    app.listen(PORT, () => {
      console.log(`🔒 Server running securely on port ${PORT}`);
    });
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('Failed to start server:', error);
    process.exit(1);
  }
}

startServer();
```

SECRETS ROTATION BEST PRACTICES
===============================

1. AUTOMATIC ROTATION (Recommended)
   ├─ Set up automatic rotation every 30-90 days
   ├─ Use secrets manager's built-in rotation
   ├─ Test rotation doesn't break application
   └─ Monitor rotation success rate

2. MANUAL ROTATION (Emergency Cases)
   ├─ Create new secret with same name (different version)
   ├─ Update secret value in secrets manager
   ├─ Verify application picks up new value
   ├─ Keep old secret for emergency rollback (1 week)
   └─ Document rotation in audit log

3. BLUE-GREEN ROTATION
   ├─ Create new secret value
   ├─ Deploy app with support for both old and new
   ├─ Switch to new secret
   ├─ Verify no errors for 1 hour
   └─ Remove old secret support

4. ZERO-DOWNTIME ROTATION
   ├─ Load both old and new secrets
   ├─ Try new secret first, fall back to old
   ├─ Monitor error rate
   ├─ Gradually phase out old secret
   └─ Remove old secret after 24+ hours

SECRETS SECURITY CHECKLIST
==========================

DO:
✓ Use a dedicated secrets manager in production
✓ Rotate critical secrets regularly (30-90 days)
✓ Use strong random values (minimum 32 bytes)
✓ Store secrets encrypted at rest and in transit
✓ Audit who accessed which secret and when
✓ Use least-privilege access (minimal IAM permissions)
✓ Enable secrets manager audit logging
✓ Use separate secrets for each environment
✓ Use separate secrets for each service/application
✓ Implement automatic secret expiration

DON'T:
✗ Store secrets in .env in production
✗ Commit .env files to version control
✗ Log secrets or display in error messages
✗ Share secrets via email or chat
✗ Use weak/predictable secret values
✗ Reuse secrets across environments
✗ Store backup copies of secrets locally
✗ Use same secret for multiple purposes
✗ Forget to rotate after incidents
✗ Skip audit logging for compliance

ENVIRONMENT-SPECIFIC SECRETS
=============================

Development:
├─ Use .env files (never committed)
├─ Short-lived secrets OK
├─ Test rotation procedures
└─ Can be less sensitive than prod

Staging:
├─ Use secrets manager (same as prod)
├─ Same secret names as production
├─ Different values from production
├─ Full audit logging enabled
└─ Test rotation procedures regularly

Production:
├─ Use secrets manager exclusively
├─ Minimal access permissions (IAM/RBAC)
├─ Automatic rotation enabled
├─ Full audit logging and monitoring
├─ Alerts on failed rotations
├─ 24/7 incident response ready
└─ Secrets marked as sensitive in manager

MONITORING & ALERTING
=====================

Set up alerts for:
├─ Failed secret retrievals
├─ Failed secret rotations
├─ Unauthorized secret access attempts
├─ Secrets accessed outside normal hours
├─ Multiple failed authentication attempts
├─ Application restart during rotation
└─ Secrets expiring soon

Example CloudWatch alerts:
```javascript
const cloudwatch = new AWS.CloudWatch();

// Alert if secret retrieval fails more than 3 times in 5 minutes
const params = {
  AlarmName: 'SecretsManager-FailedRetrievals',
